# Google Drive credentials for the weekly backup job (hitch/scripts/backup_to_drive.py).
#
# Copy to deploy/rclone.conf and fill in real values. That file is gitignored and mounted into
# the container at /root/.config/rclone/rclone.conf — it must NOT be committed and must NOT be
# baked into the image. The client secret JSON downloaded from Google Cloud Console is also
# gitignored (client_secret_*.json); it lands in the project root by default, which is a git
# repo, so check `git status` before committing after a Console download.
#
# The mount is deliberately read-write: rclone refreshes the OAuth access token roughly hourly
# and writes the new one back to this file. Verified — forcing a refresh rewrites the file.
# Mounting it :ro yields a setup that works for an hour and then breaks, i.e. it looks fine on
# the day you set it up and fails the following week.
#
# Create this file BEFORE the container next starts. Docker bind-mounts a missing source path
# by creating it as an empty *directory*, so a `docker compose up` with no rclone.conf leaves
# a root-owned directory here and rclone silently runs with no remotes configured.
#
#
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
# Setting up from scratch
# ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
#
# 1. Google Cloud Console (console.cloud.google.com), signed in as the account that owns the
#    backup Drive. Everything here is free — no billing account, no card. The Drive API quota
#    that matters is a rate limit, not a charge.
#      - Create a project (ours: "hitchwiki-maps").
#      - APIs & Services -> Library -> enable "Google Drive API".
#      - APIs & Services -> OAuth consent screen (newer Console: Google Auth Platform ->
#        Audience) -> User type "External".
#      - *** Click PUBLISH APP so the status reads "In production", NOT "Testing". ***
#        This is the one step that silently ruins the setup if skipped. While an app is in
#        Testing, Google (a) blocks anyone not added under Test users, with "has not completed
#        the Google verification process", and (b) expires issued refresh tokens after 7 DAYS.
#        This job runs every 7 days, so a Testing-mode token yields one or two good backups and
#        then months of quiet failures. Adding yourself as a Test user fixes (a) but NOT (b) —
#        publish instead.
#        You do NOT need Google's app verification. Publishing unverified is fine for an app
#        only you use; it just means an "unverified app" interstitial during login
#        (Advanced -> Go to <app> (unsafe)). "Unverified" and "Testing" are different things.
#      - Credentials -> Create Credentials -> OAuth client ID -> type "Desktop app".
#        Download the JSON.
#
#    Why our own client_id rather than blank: a blank one shares rclone's global default OAuth
#    client with every rclone user on earth, whose per-minute quota is frequently exhausted —
#    it surfaces as "Error 403: Quota exceeded ... consumer project_number:202264815644". We hit
#    that on the very first call. Our own client gets its own quota.
#
# 2. Authorize. The server is headless, so rclone cannot open a browser here. rclone's OAuth
#    callback port (53682) is fixed, and Google allows Desktop-app clients any loopback port,
#    so the registered redirect_uri (http://localhost) needs no change — ignore rclone's NOTICE
#    about it.
#
#    Easiest route, and it keeps the client secret off your laptop and out of any chat/paste
#    buffer: VS Code Remote-SSH already forwards localhost ports to this host. With VS Code
#    connected, run ON THE SERVER:
#
#      cd /var/www/maps.hitchwiki.org
#      CID=$(python3 -c "import json,glob;print(json.load(open(glob.glob('client_secret_*.json')[0]))['installed']['client_id'])")
#      CSEC=$(python3 -c "import json,glob;print(json.load(open(glob.glob('client_secret_*.json')[0]))['installed']['client_secret'])")
#      rclone authorize "drive" "$CID" "$CSEC"
#
#    It prints http://127.0.0.1:53682/auth?state=... — open that in your laptop browser; the
#    forward carries it here. Log in as the Drive-owning account, click through the unverified
#    interstitial, and rclone prints the token JSON.
#
#    (If 53682 is already taken on the laptop, `lsof -i :53682` shows what holds it. Note VS
#    Code itself may legitimately be the listener — that IS the forward, not a conflict.)
#
#    Without VS Code, either tunnel it yourself
#    (`ssh -L 53682:localhost:53682 user@host`, then run the same command on the server), or
#    run `rclone authorize` on the laptop and paste the token back. The Desktop-app client
#    secret is not treated as confidential by Google and grants nothing without user consent,
#    but the refresh token IS a live credential to the Drive — don't paste that around.
#
# 3. Assemble this file: [gdrive] block below, with client_id/client_secret from the JSON and
#    token from the authorize output. Then `chmod 600 deploy/rclone.conf`, and set in .env:
#
#      BACKUP_RCLONE_REMOTE=gdrive:hitchwiki_maps-backups
#      BACKUP_RETENTION_DAYS=28
#
# 4. Create the folder and verify:
#
#      export RCLONE_CONFIG=/var/www/maps.hitchwiki.org/deploy/rclone.conf
#      rclone mkdir gdrive:hitchwiki_maps-backups
#      rclone about gdrive:                     # proves auth
#      sudo -E python3 hitch/scripts/backup_to_drive.py --no-upload   # proves snapshot+scrub
#
#    In the container (after an image rebuild — hitch/scripts/ is baked in, not mounted):
#
#      sudo docker exec hitchhiking-map rclone lsf gdrive:hitchwiki_maps-backups
#      sudo docker exec hitchhiking-map python3 /app/hitch/scripts/backup_to_drive.py
#
# Revoking: myaccount.google.com/permissions lists granted apps. Revoking one invalidates its
# refresh token, and the next backup fails until you re-run step 2.

[gdrive]
type = drive
client_id = <from client_secret_*.json>
client_secret = <from client_secret_*.json>
scope = drive
token = {"access_token":"...","token_type":"Bearer","refresh_token":"...","expiry":"..."}
team_drive =
